WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:07.609
In 1986, the late John Hughes gave film audiences
one of his most beloved teen dramedies — and

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:08.609
teen heroes.

00:00:08.609 --> 00:00:13.429
Filled with memorable scenes, zippy one-liners,
and a motley cast of characters, Ferris Bueller's

00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:18.190
Day Off is a film that not only stands the
test of time, but helped define a genre.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:22.949
But watch this classic again as an adult,
and you realize that more than a few parts

00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:28.590
of the movie are actually pretty cartoonish,
and require a serious suspension of disbelief.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.050
Here are just a few examples.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.400
Unbelievable toys

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.989
From his his totally boss stereo to his killer
IBM computer and beyond, Ferris has a bedroom

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:40.960
that would make him the envy of any '80s teen.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.070
"Hi Ferris!

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:43.730
How's your bod?"

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:45.400
"Oh my God.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.510
You're dying?"

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:51.330
Then there's that awesome synthesizer he uses
to make convincing fart and vomit sound effects

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:56.220
while rallying the troops at school — it's
an E-mu Emulator II, the creme de la creme

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.180
of synths used by '80s bands like Depeche
Mode.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:04.171
It would have set Ferris' parents back about
eight grand — an insane price tag, considering

00:01:04.171 --> 00:01:08.530
there's no evidence Ferris even knows how
to really play it or any other instrument,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:10.360
or that his parents are quite that well-off.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:12.380
"Never had one lesson!"

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.810
Wacky wardrobe

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:22.070
Ferris changes his outfit an absurd nine times
before Cameron even picks him up in the morning,

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.150
pulling looks out of the ether like Bugs Bunny.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:29.580
All before 10:00 a.m., Ferris' assorted looks
range from PJs to pool attire, from luau gear

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:34.440
to hip jazz cat, various states of undress
coming to and from the shower, plus his blue

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.980
suit that he plans to wear to trick Mr. Rooney
into thinking he's Sloane's dad.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:39.980
Time travel

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.370
Early in the movie, Ferris and Cameron make
their phony phone call to Ed Rooney from Ferris'

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.500
house and disagree about how to handle picking
up Sloane at school.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.470
To Ferris' dismay, Cameron (as Sloane's dad)
blurts out that Mr. Rooney should have Sloane

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:53.470
outside the school ...

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:56.040
"... in ten minutes by herself!"

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.550
This leads to a series of events that would
suspiciously take longer than 10 minutes:

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:04.150
Ferris and Cameron argue over the details,
Ferris delivers an emotional apology, and

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:05.600
the two are chums again.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.109
They then travel to Cameron's house, where
Ferris convinces Cameron to "borrow" Cameron's

00:02:10.109 --> 00:02:11.950
dad's vintage Ferrari.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.909
They secure the vehicle and travel all the
way to Shermer High, somehow arriving only

00:02:15.909 --> 00:02:18.920
a minute or so after Mr. Rooney and Sloane
emerge.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:20.000
Identity crisis

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.150
The biggest mystery about Sloane Peterson
is how seamlessly she goes from utter terror

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:29.370
that Ferris' dad might spot her in the taxi
line at the restaurant to nonchalance about

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:33.580
making kissy faces to Mr. Bueller later from
the backseat of another taxi cab.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:38.659
Sure, she has those Ray-Ban sunglasses on
as a brilliant disguise, but even perpetually

00:02:38.659 --> 00:02:43.900
oblivious Mr. Bueller must recognize his son's
super-serious girlfriend, right?

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.659
Considering Ferris proposed to Sloane back
at the Stock Exchange, things might get pretty

00:02:47.659 --> 00:02:49.470
awkward at the wedding.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.609
Mysterious gifts

00:02:50.609 --> 00:02:55.280
Aside from a quick visit from Mrs. Bueller
to check on an ailing Ferris, and Ferris'

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.950
sister Jeanie's time at home when she kicks
Mr. Rooney in the face and huddles in her

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:03.390
bedroom to call the police, the Bueller home
is mostly empty for the day while Ferris enjoys

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:04.390
his time off.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:08.260
Yet when everyone comes home at 6:00 p.m.,
the hallways are filled to the rafters with

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:13.249
the balloon and flower deliveries sent by
the entire town to wish Ferris a speedy recovery.

00:03:13.249 --> 00:03:15.700
So who accepted all those deliveries?

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:19.170
It couldn't have been Jeanie — in fact,
we see her slam the door in the singing telegram

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.170
nurse's face.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:24.440
And it likely wasn't Mr. Rooney, because the
one vase of flowers we saw him pick up ended

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.430
up on the head of the family's Rottweiler.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.090
Does Ferris have a robot butler among his
many fabulous toys?

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.370
The late bus

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:36.120
Ferris Bueller's Day Off is loaded with brilliant
moments and immensely quotable lines, but

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.129
the fun doesn't stop when the credits roll.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:42.380
You've gotta love seeing the disheveled and
broken Mr. Rooney accepting a warm gummy bear

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:47.680
from his bespectacled bus seatmate, set to
the unforgettably catchy strains of Yello's

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:49.720
"Oh Yeah."

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.460
"I bet you've never smelled a real school
bus before."

00:03:52.469 --> 00:03:57.000
But when you watch it again as an adult, you
realize there's something peculiar about that

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.519
school bus filled with teens pulling up to
Mr. Rooney and asking him if he needs a ride.

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:06.370
We know it's now long past 6:00 p.m., and
those kids should have been home hours ago.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:07.409
Who are these kids?

00:04:07.409 --> 00:04:09.110
Is the driver lost?

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.680
Considering everything else he managed to
pull off, sticking Rooney on this bizarre

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.060
bus just might be Ferris' handiwork.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.010
"It's good for him.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.320
It teaches him to deal with his fear."

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:23.000
Thanks for watching!

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.850
